<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ernest II, Duke of Swabia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1010–<a href="/years/1030">1030</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-babenberg">House of Babenberg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ernest II (c. 1010–1030) was Duke of Swabia from 1015 and a stepson of Emperor Conrad II. He rebelled against Conrad in 1025–1027, claiming the inheritance of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, and was imprisoned. Refusing to act against his ally Werner of Kyburg, he was deposed and outlawed, and both were killed in 1030. His story helped inspire the medieval epic Herzog Ernst.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ernest was a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-babenberg">House of Babenberg</a>, the son of Duke Ernest I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> and Gisela of Swabia. He succeeded his father in 1015 as a minor, so the duchy was governed by regents: first his mother, and later his uncle Poppo, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>. In 1016 Gisela married Conrad, who was elected King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1024 as Conrad II. Conrad treated his stepson generously at first, but Ernest resented the power of the German kings over Swabia, and the two became rivals for the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, whose childless king, Rudolph III, was the brother of Gisela's mother Gerberga.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ernest rose against Conrad in 1025 while still young. Conrad had overcome the resistance by 1026, and Ernest submitted and received the abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kempten">Kempten</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>. During Conrad's Italian expedition of 1026 and 1027 he joined the opposition again, but the local Swabian lords refused to support him, and he was forced to surrender and imprisoned. Gisela backed her husband but did not wish her son wholly humiliated, so Ernest kept the title of duke, though she probably governed the duchy while he was held.</p><p>After Conrad's son Henry was crowned king in 1028, Ernest was offered his duchy back if he gave up his vassal Werner of Kyburg, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> diet of 1030 the offer was made again on condition that he act against Conrad's enemies. His refusal, above all to turn on his friend Werner, brought his final fall: he was deposed and placed under the imperial ban. A few months later both men were killed fighting the troops of Count Manegold in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a>, Ernest on 17 August 1030. Ernest was buried in Konstanz Minster, and the duchy passed to his younger brother Herman.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ernest II of Swabia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The castle of Kyburg near Winterthur belonged to the counts of Winterthur in the early 11th century; in 1027 it was besieged by Emperor Conrad II during the revolt of Duke <b>Ernest II of Swabia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-kyburg">County of Kyburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Ruine Falkenstein, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,8.38&amp;year=1023" aria-label="Ruine Falkenstein on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Ernest II, Duke of Swabia"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5476 5971h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5476 5971h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,8.38&amp;year=1023">What was here in 1023? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
